# The sum of two intervals with one-limb bounds (milestone2.md, "Interval kernels", "One limb"):
# the lower bounds added downward, the upper bounds upward, so that the result is the smallest
# interval of T that contains the exact range; the empty set when an operand is empty (its lower
# bound is then +inf). Bounded exactly when the exact range is within Omega, for bounded operands.
kernel:        interval_add_1
input:         stored xl
input:         stored xu
input:         stored yl
input:         stored yu
output:        zl
output:        zu
requires:      interval xl xu
requires:      interval yl yu
ensures:       encloses_interval zl zu x+y x=xl,xu y=yl,yu tight=rounded bounded_when=abs_le(x+y,omega) bounded_exactly
min_precision: 2
source:        directed sums of the bounds (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeInterval1.v interval_add_1_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the bounds are directed sums, which select, and the empty set is selected after an explicit test; encloses_interval states what comes out

l  = add_down_1(xl, yl)
h  = add_up_1(xu, yu)
nx = lt(xl, const inf)
ny = lt(yl, const inf)
ne = and(nx, ny)
mi = neg(const inf)
zl = select(ne, l, const inf)
zu = select(ne, h, mi)
